export const DOCS_METHOD_TEXT: Record<string, string> = {
  "frequency": "## Frequency\nAtlas calculates scheduled headways: the time between consecutive departures. It reads departure times from stop_times.txt and expands valid frequency-based trips from frequencies.txt; it does not treat that optional table as a single route-level answer.\nDepartures are grouped by route, direction, day and service period. A route’s line colour and filter result come from the shared stretch, where all of a direction’s branches run, in its slowest direction. Each branch is read on its own and does not set the route’s result. Where the branches share no common stretch, as at a hub, the slowest one counts. Stop-level cadence is calculated separately. For a group of departures, Atlas sorts the consecutive gaps and uses the median gap as the typical wait. On route cards, when a rider arriving at a random time would wait at least 10 min longer than the median, the card shows that wait instead. It is read at midday, or else the PM peak, or else across the day, and only where the route runs through that time. The frequency filter and map colours still use the median.\nOne odd gap does not change the median, so one unusually long gap does not make otherwise regular service every 10 min look like service every 30 min. The exception is service where short and long gaps alternate (for example gaps of 60, 120, 60 min): the median would show only the short gap and halve the real wait, so Atlas reads the longer wait instead.\nPeriod calculations are kept inside their own time windows. Atlas does not silently borrow an average from across the day to fill a period with sparse or missing service.\nAtlas keeps separate projections for display cadence, filter cadence, branch cadence, shared-stretch cadence and stop-specific cadence. A stop or shared stretch can have a different value from the whole route because it may combine branches or cover only part of the route.\nPeriod output also carries separate longest-gap and sustained-service signals. These diagnostics can mark a median as unrepresentative without changing the stored median or silently filling the gap with a value from across the day.\n## Weekday Schedules\nAtlas evaluates Monday through Friday separately. It does not combine departures from different weekdays into one synthetic schedule, because that can create service that never actually ran on any single day.\nThe frequency tier is the slowest tier among the weekdays the route runs as a regular schedule; a day that only holds the tail of the previous night, or a short burst of a few trips, does not set it. The displayed cadence uses the actual weekday whose trip count is closest to the median weekday trip count, so it represents a real day without letting an unusually busy or unusually quiet day dominate the summary.\nA route that runs on only one or two weekdays is shown as limited on weekdays instead of borrowing a frequency from those days. Weekday stop and time-of-day numbers all describe the same typical weekday.\n## Directions, Branches and Route Qualification\nAtlas calculates each scheduled direction separately before deciding whether a route qualifies. It uses the feed’s direction_id when present. If that field is missing, trips fall into the default direction group, Atlas records a warning and the resulting headway may be understated because opposite directions may have been merged.\nWithin a direction, Atlas also separates different headsigns and physical shapes so branches and short turns do not get mixed into one artificially frequent series. One exception: when two patterns take turns running trips to the same end of the line, and neither runs consistently on its own but together they do, Atlas reads them as one service on the stops they share. Every route, whatever its mode, keeps the longest meaningful shape groups for analysis, with a trip-volume fallback when length alone would discard most of the service. These are analysis choices, not claims that the feed’s direction labels are always correct.\nAtlas finds branches from the trips themselves: a new branch starts where the set of regular trips serving the stops changes, however few stops it covers. One-off and irregular trips don’t start a branch.\nOn a route card, the shared stretch is one line and each branch is its own. A branch whose trips are too few or unevenly spaced reads “Irregular”, and one that runs only part of a period says when it starts or stops.\nThe frequency filter and the map colours are direction-aware. For a period threshold, every direction must meet the threshold, and the worst direction decides, so a route does not pass solely because one direction is frequent while the other is materially slower. A rush-hour-only route with consistent trips is rated by its own spacing, and passes the filter for a time period only when its trips run the whole period, with the waits before its first trip and after its last counted. A route that runs for only part of a period shows the hours it runs, and says when it has no trips or runs less often the rest of the time. Occasional extra trips, such as a short turn or an extension with only a few runs, are set aside when the same direction has a consistent main pattern, and a direction is never dropped from the check. For example, a direction every 10 min paired with one every 30 min does not pass ≤10 min; it can pass ≤30 min if the other route-level checks also pass. The separate “All” service view is broader by design: it can show service in only one direction or service that has not qualified for a numeric tier. A route/day with a direction that has no sustained, real-tier pattern can also be marked occasional as a whole and hidden by the default occasional-service filter.\n## Band Qualification and Tolerance\nThis band test decides whether a pattern counts as consistent service, as opposed to infrequent or occasional service. Atlas tests the tightest band first and requires enough departures to cover the analysed span at that band. For a target of T minutes, the minimum trip count is based on ceil(span ÷ T).\nA gap at or below T passes directly. A slightly longer internal gap can count as a near miss when it is no more than T + max(5 min, 15% of T). The number of allowed near misses is tier-specific and capped at three:\nTarget band | Near-miss limit | Allowance\n10 min | 15 min | 10% of gaps\n15 min | 20 min | 10% of gaps\n20 min | 25 min | 5% of gaps\n30 min | 35 min | 5% of gaps\n60 min | 69 min | 5% of gaps\nAtlas rounds the percentage down, but allows at least one near miss once there are 8 or more gaps, and never more than three. A first or last gap that is genuinely at the edge of service may receive a separate allowance up to T + 10 min. That edge allowance also uses one of the same near-miss slots, and at least four departures must remain after the edge treatment is applied. A gap beyond the relevant limit, or too many near misses, disqualifies the band and Atlas tests the next slower one.\nThe wait from the start of a time period to its first trip, and from its last trip to the period’s end, counts like any other gap. A route that starts late or stops early in a period no longer passes the filter for it on the gaps between its trips alone. Where it is the true start or end of the day’s service, the wait gets the edge allowance described above.\nThese allowances handle ordinary schedule rounding and small deviations without turning a genuinely uneven route into a frequent one. A large gap is still a failure even if the median looks good, and the final tier is the fastest band that survives the full check. Every mode, bus, rail and streetcar alike, is graded on the same 5, 8, 10, 15, 20, 30 and 60 min bands; the 5 and 8 min bands count as 10 and are not separate options in the main map filter.\n## Frequency Bands\nThe main Frequency control currently exposes service at ≤10, ≤15, ≤20, ≤30 and ≤60 min. The filter and the line colour use the route’s worst-direction cadence, so a route whose slowest direction runs less often than every 60 min appears as infrequent. Occasional service is hidden by default.\n## Sustained Service and Long Gaps\nA median alone can hide an important gap. For example, departures might be every 30 min during a period except for one 180 min gap. Atlas keeps additional coverage and longest-gap signals so a route can be marked as uneven instead of presenting the median as the whole story.\nFrequency tiers therefore require sustained service across the relevant window. A route does not qualify for a fast tier simply because it has a short burst of frequent trips at one edge of the period.\nWhen a route operates only during part of the day, Atlas records that absence as an empty period rather than copying its operating-period value into the rest of the day.",
  "service": "## Service\nFrequency and regularity are separate properties. A route can run every 30 min and still be occasional if it only operates for a short burst or has too few repeated departures to establish a sustained pattern.\nRegular: Service covers the normal analysis window. It may be frequent or infrequent.\nPart-time: Only at rush hour, evenings or some days.\nOccasional: School runs, a few trips a day, on-demand.\nTwo filters, both on by default, decide which of these routes the map shows. Hide occasional routes hides occasional service: school runs, a few trips a day, on-demand. Hide part-time routes hides part-time service: only at rush hour, evenings or some days. A part-time route you choose to show is still judged by the frequency filter, on the selected period’s frequency. Periods outside its operation remain empty rather than inheriting a value from across the day.\nRoutes with only a few trips in a period read “Irregular” instead of a frequency, and route cards name each pattern, such as “Rush hour only”. Each filter links to a list of the routes it hides.",
  "figures": "## Mapping\nThe map is a React application using MapLibre for map interaction and rendering. Route geometry is distributed in vector tiles for broad map views, while agency detail and stop indexes are loaded as smaller JSON files when needed.\nAt a high level, the browser combines three kinds of information:\n- agency metadata, coverage, source dates and quality notices;\n- route geometry and serialized frequency properties; and\n- stop coordinates and route-specific service metrics.\nThis separation keeps the initial map responsive and lets Atlas update schedule data independently from the application interface.\n## Why Values Can Differ Across the Interface\nAtlas exposes several legitimate views of service:\n- Display cadence describes the route or destination a person is looking at.\n- Filter cadence is the worst direction’s cadence. It decides whether a route qualifies for a selected frequency threshold and sets the line colour and the dot colour in the route chooser. Route lists, agency and area pages, Compare and search show the filter’s value, so a number in a list always passes the filter at that number. Where a route doesn’t pass the filter’s check in a period, they show “Irregular” instead of a number.\n- Stop cadence describes service at a specific boarding stop.\n- Shared-stretch cadence describes the combined service where branches overlap.\nThese projections answer different questions. A stop’s value is for display only: a busy stop never makes a route qualify on its own. The shared stretch is read for the route’s rating, as the Frequency section explains. When a route already qualifies, the map can highlight the stretch where stop-level service meets the threshold. Atlas labels the surfaces rather than pretending there is one universal number.\n## Public Outputs\nAfter processing, Atlas publishes a set of read-only files for the map:\n- route geometry with serialized service metrics;\n- a lightweight stop index for searching and stop-specific views;\n- trip and stop-metadata files published for downstream tools;\n- vector tiles for efficient map rendering; and\n- agency metadata describing coverage, freshness, sources and quality status.\nThese files contain processed public transit information. They do not contain user accounts, private analytics records, feed credentials or the raw maintainer workflow.",
  "quality": "## Quality\nAtlas checks feeds for structural and service-data problems, including missing or unusual geometry, schedule inconsistencies, stale sources and route-frequency anomalies. Some known source problems are corrected or annotated before publication.\nFeed-specific corrections are kept separate from the general methodology so a fix for one agency does not silently change every agency’s results.\n## A Conservative Publication Rule\nAtlas would rather show no result and clearly indicate that data is missing than show a precise-looking result that may be wrong. This applies to route geometry, frequency metrics, service areas and research findings.\nWhen the evidence is incomplete, Atlas can leave a route or field out, mark a result as unavailable or provisional, keep a feed in review, or keep the last good published data with a freshness warning. It does not fill gaps with a guessed value merely to make the map look complete.\n## How Atlas Communicates Data Quality\nQuality decisions are not meant to disappear behind a clean-looking map. Atlas surfaces user-relevant changes in the interface:\n- Corrected data: agency and route cards can say “We corrected an error in this route’s schedule.” with an explanation of the known feed problem and technical issue links when available.\n- Outdated schedules: route cards say “[Agency]’s schedule ended on [date]. Times shown may be out of date.”\n- New schedule data: for agencies whose past feeds have repeatedly had problems, a newly published feed is marked “This route’s new schedule is still being verified.” until Atlas checks it for missing service and quality problems.\n- Feed quality: quality controls distinguish Healthy, Review, Degraded and Unusable feeds. The reasons can include errors reading the feed, validation warnings, adjusted shapes, route-versus-stop frequency differences, expired schedules or no usable route output.\n- Route-level warnings: a route can carry a separate warning when its displayed geometry needed adjustment or its service data needs interpretation.\nNot every internal exclusion becomes a separate banner. Clearly unusable rows can be removed during processing without overwhelming the map with implementation details, while known user-facing corrections, stale schedules, review states and material quality problems remain labelled where they affect interpretation.\n## How Outdated Data Is Flagged\nAtlas determines schedule freshness from the service dates inside the feed, not only from the date the file was downloaded. It compares the latest usable date in feed_info.txt, calendar.txt, and non-removal entries in calendar_dates.txt with the current date, and an agency with several feeds uses the earliest of its feeds’ dates. A schedule also counts as ended when the calendars that carry most of its service have ended, even if a few rows run on.\nDuring each refresh, Atlas records the feed’s expiry or version. If every available feed has expired, a download fails or validation fails, Atlas marks the agency as stale and retains the last good published data instead of replacing it with broken or empty output.\nThis also applies when a source publishes a newer GTFS file that is malformed or fails Atlas’s processing checks: the newer file is not promoted simply because it is newer. The active feed identity and last successful refresh remain unchanged; Atlas records the reason for the failure and tries again at the next refresh. If a source’s file is empty or unreadable, Atlas tries the agency’s next source for that feed.\nThe interface then identifies that schedule as potentially outdated and shows the known end date and the check history when available. For agencies whose past feeds have repeatedly had problems, a newer feed is marked as under review while it is checked for missing service and other quality problems. A feed can therefore be visible, stale, under review, degraded or unusable as separate states rather than being silently treated as current.\nSource feeds can be delayed, incomplete, discontinued or incorrect.",
  "glossary": "## Glossary\nBranch: One of several paths under one route number, such as trips that split to different destinations or turn back early. The route card lists each one with its own frequency.\nConsistent service: Service whose gaps stay within a small allowance across the whole time period, not only in a short burst.\n“Every N min”: The time between trips in the selected period. A range such as “every 8–25 min” means your wait is between the two: on a card it’s one row’s waits, and in a list it runs from the shared stretch to the least frequent branch.\nFrequency: How often a route runs. Atlas shows what the schedule promises, not what happened on the street.\nFrequency tier: A broad band, such as ≤10, ≤15, ≤20, ≤30 or ≤60 min, used to colour and filter routes on the map. The filter and the legend show it as “≤10 min”.\nGTFS: A standard set of files describing transit routes, stops, trips, calendars and shapes. Agencies publish it, and Atlas reads it.\nGTFS-Flex: GTFS extensions for demand-responsive service, including service areas, location groups, virtual stops and booking rules.\nHeadway: The time between one scheduled departure and the next. For a rider who just missed a bus, it is roughly the wait for the next one.\nIrregular: Shown for a time period when a route runs but its trips don’t come at a consistent gap, so there is no frequency to give. Route cards, tables and lists say “Irregular”.\nOccasional: School runs, a few trips a day, on-demand. “Hide occasional routes” hides them.\nPMTiles: A single archive format that lets the map request only the vector-tile regions it needs.\nProvenance: The source, schedule period, freshness state, review status and correction history attached to an Atlas result.\nRoute rating: A route’s line colour and filter result come from the shared stretch, where all of a direction’s branches run, in its slowest direction. Each branch is read on its own and does not set the route’s result. Where the branches share no common stretch, as at a hub, the slowest one counts.\nService class: The kind of service a route runs: regular, part-time or occasional.\nService period: A named time window: AM peak (6–9 AM), Midday (9 AM–3 PM), PM peak (3–7 PM), Evening (7–11 PM), Late (11 PM–2 AM), or Overnight (2–6 AM). Each period is measured on its own. A quiet evening is not hidden by a busy midday.\nShared stretch: The part of a route where its branches run together, and riders there get every branch’s trips. The card shows it as “Shared stretch”. It sets the route’s colour and filter result, and each branch is read on its own.\nUneven service: Service that runs through the whole time period, but with gaps that vary.",
  "live": "Late is more than 5½ min behind schedule, unless the agency publishes its own limit, which we use instead.\nTrip time is how long a typical trip takes over the main stretch, compared with the schedule.\nExtra wait is how much longer riders wait than the schedule says, worked out from the gaps between vehicles at each stop.\nBunching is when the gap between vehicles is under 2 min, or more than twice the scheduled gap.\nDelivered is the share of scheduled trips that actually ran.\nVehicle positions are recorded once a minute for each vehicle. The time it left each stop is estimated from those and compared with the schedule.\nTrip matching links each vehicle to its scheduled trip by trip number.\nAgency figures combine every trip on every covered route. A route with more trips has more effect on the total.\nStops are listed once they have 30 or more departures, with up to 3 per route direction.\nColoured changes are bigger than the figure’s normal day-to-day range. Grey changes fall inside that range and may just be normal variation.\nRecording gaps are times our recording missed, such as when the agency’s vehicle locations stopped coming in. Trips during a gap aren’t counted, and days with gaps are left out.\nTypical deviation: 1+ min ahead · On time · 1–3 min · 3+ min behind"
};
